    viva: [
      { q: "Why does osteomyelitis occur in the metaphysis in children?", a: "The metaphyseal capillary loops make hairpin turns with sluggish blood flow, which allows bacteria to settle; the physis acts as a barrier so infection stays in the metaphysis in children over 1 year, while in neonates it may cross the physis into the epiphysis and joint." },
      { q: "What are the common organisms in bone infection?", a: "Staphylococcus aureus (commonest), Streptococcus, Haemophilus influenzae (less common after Hib vaccine), Salmonella (sickle cell disease), Pseudomonas (IV drug use, foot puncture), Mycobacterium tuberculosis, and Gram negatives in neonates and elderly." },
      { q: "What investigations help in acute osteomyelitis?", a: "Raised ESR, CRP and WBC; blood culture and aspirate culture; X-ray shows soft tissue swelling early and periosteal reaction after 10 to 14 days; MRI is the earliest sensitive investigation; bone scan if MRI is unavailable." },
      { q: "How do you treat acute osteomyelitis?", a: "Rest and splint the limb, IV antibiotics (anti-staphylococcal such as cloxacillin or cefazolin, with MRSA cover as required) for 2 weeks then oral to complete 6 weeks, and surgical drainage if there is a subperiosteal abscess or no response in 48 to 72 hours." },
      { q: "What are sequestrum, involucrum and cloaca?", a: "Sequestrum: dead segment of bone. Involucrum: new bone formed around it by the periosteum. Cloaca: the hole in the involucrum through which pus drains to the sinus. Chronic osteomyelitis needs surgical sequestrectomy and debridement." },
      { q: "How do you manage septic arthritis?", a: "Urgent joint aspiration (or arthrotomy and washout in hip and in children), IV antibiotics, splintage, then early mobilisation; delay can destroy the joint and cause avascular necrosis of the femoral head." },
    ],
